<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We deal with the problem of decomposing a complete geometric graph into plane star-forests. In particular, we disprove a recent conjecture by Pach, Saghafian and Schnider by constructing for each <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> a complete geometric graph on <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices which can be decomposed into <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests. Additionally we prove that for even <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, every decomposition of complete abstract graph on <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices into <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests is composed of a perfect matching and <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests with two edge-balanced components, which we call broken double stars.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A classic question asked in graph theory is the following:
“Given a graph <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, what is the minimal number of subgraphs with property <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> that the edges of <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned into?”
Historically, this question was asked for abstract graphs and property <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> was replaced with forests, trees, complete bipartite graphs and many more <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
Similar questions can be asked about graphs drawn in the plane or on any other surface. Here we want to decompose a complete graph on the surface into subgraphs that have a certain geometric property in addition to the property <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Answering such questions is a similar, but separate research direction that has been pursued by many authors in discrete geometry and graph drawing communities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">geometric</em> graph is a graph drawn in the plane, with vertices represented by points in general position and edges as straight line segments between them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, there has been a lot of work done on decomposing geometric graphs into planar subgraphs of a special kind, such as trees, stars, double stars etc. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. This paper will be concerned with decomposing complete geometric graphs into plane star-forests.
A <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">star</em> is a connected graph on <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> vertices with one vertex of degree <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> (center) and <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices of degree <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Our definition allows a graph that has two vertices and a single edge to be a star but it is not clear what the center should be. In this case we define the center to be one of the endpoints of the edge, and this choice is arbitrary. The definition also allows that a single vertex is a star and in this case this vertex is also the center of said star. A <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">star-forest</em> is a forest whose every connected component is a star. It is easy to observe that a complete graph <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> stars. Furthermore, <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be decomposed into less than <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> stars <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. In the same paper, Akiyama and Kano proved that <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into at most <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil\frac{n}{2}\rceil+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil\frac{n}{2}\rceil+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests and that this bound is tight.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The story is different for complete geometric graphs.
To the best of our knowledge, the first mention of star-forest decompositions was made by Dujmović and Wood in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. They asked if one can decompose a complete geometric graph on <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, whose vertices form a convex polygon into less than <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, this question was ansewered in the negative by Pach, Saghafian and Schnider <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. They showed that a complete geometric graph whose vertices form a convex polygon cannot be decomposed into fewer than <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests.
In the same paper, the authors posed the following question, which they considered the most important one in this direction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmquestion1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmquestion1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmquestion1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmquestion1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What is the minimal number of plane star-forests that a complete geometric graph can be decomposed into?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Based on their findings they made the following conjecture:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. There is no complete geometric graph with <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices that can be decomposed into fewer than <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil 3n/4\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil 3n/4\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The main aim of this note is to answer this conjecture in the negative.
The authors in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> give a special configuration of <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4k</annotation></semantics></math> points and construct a simple decomposition into <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3n/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3n/4</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests. Motivated by this example, we first describe a method generalizing it. This is the content of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 ‣ 3 Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>
Then we provide a point set on <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math> points which can be decomposed into <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2n}{3}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2n}{3}=4</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests, disproving the conjecture.
We then improve the bound further by constructing complete geometric graphs which can be decomposed into <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests, which is best possible. This is the content of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 ‣ 3 Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.
Attacking this problem raised some further questions regarding decompositions of abstract complete graphs into star-forests. Mainly, our computations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> have shown us that for <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6,8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6,8</annotation></semantics></math>, the decomposition of <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests is unique in a certain sense. In each fitting decomposition one star-forest was a perfect matching on <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> vertices while the other <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests were edge balanced, spanning and had centers at endpoints of an edge of the matching. We call such a decomposition a broken double stars decomposition.
We prove that for <math id="S1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> even, every decomposition of the abstract complete graph <math id="S1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests is a broken double stars decomposition. This is the content of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 ‣ 2 Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section our goal is to define a unique construction of star-forest decompositions of the complete graph. These star-forests will be a byproduct of a decomposition of <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into special trees, called <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">double stars</em>. A double star is a graph composed of two vertex-disjoint stars, whose centers are joined by an edge. For an even <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the double star decomposition of <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained in a following way. Let <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a perfect matching in <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for each edge <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in M</annotation></semantics></math> we create a double star by connecting each endpoint of <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n-2}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n-2}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> vertices of <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in such a way that we do not obtain a cycle. This results in a decomposition of the edge set into <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> double stars.
From this we can define a decomposition of <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into star-forests in the most natural way. One forest is a matching on <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> edges, and each of the other <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> forests is composed of two stars with <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> edges each, whose centers are the endpoints of an edge of the matching. This construction was described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and again in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. For a visual explanation see Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We will call any such a decomposition of <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">broken double stars</em> decomposition. We now formally state the main result of this section.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.11044v1/broken_star_forest.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:119/140;" width="119" height="140" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>A broken double star decomposition of <math id="S2.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{6}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2k</annotation></semantics></math> be an even integer. Then any decomposition of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests is a broken double star decomposition.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">But before proving this we need a couple of building blocks. We first note that we can assume that a decomposition of <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2k}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests cannot contain a star forest with a single component. This is because removing the center of this component and all of the adjacent edges would result in a decomposition of <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;\lceil\frac{2k-1}{2}\rceil+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;\lceil\frac{2k-1}{2}\rceil+1</annotation></semantics></math> star forests. Which is impossible by the result of Akiyama and Kano <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2k</annotation></semantics></math> be an even integer and let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0},\dots,F_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0},\dots,F_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a decomposition of <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests. If <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a matching then every other star-forest is spanning and consists of two components.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that we have such a decomposition and that one of the star-forests has at least three components. Then it can have at most <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k-3</annotation></semantics></math> edges. Then the number of edges covered by the decomposition is at most <math id="Thmproofx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+2k-3+(k-1)(2k+2)&lt;k(2k-1)=\binom{2k}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+2k-3+(k-1)(2k+2)&lt;k(2k-1)=\binom{2k}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2k</annotation></semantics></math> be an even integer and let <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> be a decomposition of <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests. If some <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is a matching then this decomposition is a broken double stars decomposition.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us label the vertices of <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that we have a decomposition of <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into star-forests such that one of the forests is a matching. Assume without loss of generality that the edges of the matching are given by <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i},v_{i+k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i},v_{i+k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we will prove that for each forest <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}\neq F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}\neq F</annotation></semantics></math> there exists an <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that the centers of the stars in star-forest <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i},v_{i+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i},v_{i+k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume for contradiction that there is a star-forest <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in the decomposition whose stars have centers <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i\pm k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>±</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i\pm k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then consider the star-forest with center in <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i+k}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> be the center of the other star in this forest. Assume that <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is spanning, and thus that <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i+k},v_{j}\}\in E(F^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i+k},v_{j}\}\in E(F^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> and without loss of generality <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u,v_{j}\}\in E(F^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u,v_{j}\}\in E(F^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>. But, since <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i},v_{i+k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i},v_{i+k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge in the matching it means that the forest with centers <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i+k},u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i+k},u</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be spanning, contradicting Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 ‣ 2 Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Lastly, we need to prove that inside of each star-forest, stars have equal number of edges. For this we will assume that the forest with centers in <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i},v_{i+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i},v_{i+k}</annotation></semantics></math> does not have stars with equal number of edges. Assume that in this star-forest <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected to vertices <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i+1},\dots,v_{i+k-1},v_{i+k+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i+1},\dots,v_{i+k-1},v_{i+k+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i+k}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected with <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i+k+2},\dots,v_{2k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i+k+2},\dots,v_{2k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then both of the edges <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i+k},v_{i+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i+k},v_{i+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i+k},v_{i+k+1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i+k},v_{i+k+1}\}</annotation></semantics></math> need to be used by the star-forest with appropriate centers, which is clearly impossible.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following observation has been checked computationally.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 2.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any decomposition of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests is a broken double stars decomposition.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally we proceed with a proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 ‣ 2 Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span><span id="Thmproofx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 ‣ 2 Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will proceed by induction on <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. For the base case <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> the claim holds by Observation <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Observation 2.2 ‣ 2 Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Now assume that it holds for <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose that we have a decomposition of <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2k}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests. If any star-forest has a single component which is not a single vertex, removing the center of that component leaves us with a decomposition of <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests, which is impossible as <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;\lceil\frac{2k-1}{2}\rceil+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;\lceil\frac{2k-1}{2}\rceil+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Further if each star-forest has at least three components, we cannot cover all of the edges since each component can have at most <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k-3</annotation></semantics></math> edges and <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)(2k-3)&lt;\binom{2k+1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)(2k-3)&lt;\binom{2k+1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore there is at least one star-forest with two components. Consider the graph obtained by removing the centers (call them <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,d</annotation></semantics></math> of such a star-forest from <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2k}</annotation></semantics></math>. We are then left with a decomposition of <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2k-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2k-2}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests. By the inductive hypothesis, this must be a broken double star decomposition. One of the edges removed was the one between the two centers, call it <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to a forest with centers <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the edges between <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be in the same forest as <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. So assume that two of these edges, <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,c\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{d,b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{d,b\}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to another forest with centers <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime},b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime},b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, without loss of generality, the edges <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,a^{\prime}\},\{b,b^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,a^{\prime}\},\{b,b^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> belong to the forest with centers <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b</annotation></semantics></math> and the edges <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,b^{\prime}\},\{b,a^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,b^{\prime}\},\{b,a^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> must belong to a forest different than the one with centers <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\prime},b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\prime},b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the inductive assumption. Therefore, <math id="Thmproofx3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> must extend the matching in the decomposition of the smaller graph and the result follows by Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 ‣ 2 Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Firstly, we will describe a generalization of the method from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and use it to construct a counterexample. After this we construct a complete geometric graph on <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> vertices which can be decomposed into <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests. From now on, we will write <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GP</annotation></semantics></math> for a complete geometric graph whose underlying point set is <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in(1/2,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in(1/2,1)</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant. If there is a complete geometric graph on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> points which can be partitioned into <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cn_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cn_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests, in such a way that each vertex is a center of at least one tree, then for each integer <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a complete geometric graph on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kn_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kn_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> points that can be partitioned into <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ckn_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ckn_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be the underlying point set of the original complete geometric graph and let <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer. Label the points in <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\dots,a_{n_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\dots,a_{n_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, replace each <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by a set <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=\{a_{i}^{1},\dots,a_{i}^{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=\{a_{i}^{1},\dots,a_{i}^{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> points in general position in such a way that if we choose <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1},\dots,b_{n_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1},\dots,b_{n_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\in A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\in A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a point set of the same order type as <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Call the new point set <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now if <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1},\dots,F_{cn_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1},\dots,F_{cn_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the decomposition of <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GS</annotation></semantics></math> into plane star-forests, from this, we will obtain the decomposition of <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GS^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GS^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(kn_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(kn_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests.
Let <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the center of a star in <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will construct <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> new stars with centers in <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{1},\dots,a_{j}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{1},\dots,a_{j}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Start with <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, add to it all of the edges of the form <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{j}^{1},a_{j}^{l}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{j}^{1},a_{j}^{l}\}</annotation></semantics></math> that were not already used (in the case of <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, none were used). Now for each edge of the form <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{j},a_{j^{\prime}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{j},a_{j^{\prime}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, add all of the edges from <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to the vertices in <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{j^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{j^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Continue doing this for each vertex <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\{1,2,\dots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\{1,2,\dots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We do this for each star in <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and for each forest in the original decomposition. The result of this process is <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cn_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cn_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> families of star-forests, each of size <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. And the planarity of the star-forests follows from the definition of the point set <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, assume that a tree in the new decomposition has an intersection. Then the intersection is between edges whose <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> vertices are in different <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s. But if this was the case, then a choice of transversal that includes this <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> vertices would induce a crossing inside the original decomposition of <math id="Thmproofx4.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GS</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We note that the assumption that each point is a center of at least one forest is crucial as otherwise the star-forests constructed in the proof do not cover all of the edges.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
