<td id="S3.F2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><img src="2402.11044v1/4x1.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:129/92;" width="129" height="92" alt="Refer to caption"> <span id="S3.F2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">aaaa</span>
<img src="2402.11044v1/4x3.png" id="S3.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:135/99;" width="135" height="99" alt="Refer to caption"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>A complete geometric graph on <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> vertices decomposed into three plane star-forests and the corresponding graph on <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> vertices with the decomposition into <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests (only <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> are drawn for readability). Each vertex of the point set on the left has been used as a center of one tree and colored accordingly.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 ‣ 3 Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> gives us a nice way of constructing infinitely many complete geometric graphs that can be partitioned into few plane star-forests, we still need concrete small examples to be able to produce the infinitudes. One example was given by the authors in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and can be found in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. This example motivated Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1 ([]) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We proceed in a similar fashion.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a configuration of <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> points in the plane which can be partitioned into <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests in such a way that each point is a center of at least one star.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a configuration of <math id="Thmproofx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> points which is crossing-minimal according to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. We decompose the graph into <math id="Thmproofx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests as in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The graph has thus been decomposed into three 2-component star-forests colored blue, red and black and one 3-component forest colored purple.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Now, using the point set of <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}=6</annotation></semantics></math> elements from the above lemma, which can be decomposed into <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n_{0}/3=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n_{0}/3=4</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests, we obtain as an easy corollary a family of point sets on <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6k</annotation></semantics></math> points which can be decomposed into <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n/3</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests, thus disproving Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1 ([]) ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We state this formally below.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> divisible by <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a geometric graph on <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices which can be decomposed into <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n/3</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.11044v1/6points.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:126/97;" width="126" height="97" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>A complete geometric graph on <math id="S3.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> vertices decomposed into four star-forests, vertices are colored the same as trees whose centers they are.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For every <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbf{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐍</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbf{N}</annotation></semantics></math> we construct a point set on <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> points that can be decomposed into <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests. By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 ‣ 2 Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, one star-forest in the decomposition will be a matching. So our approach will be to first construct the matching as an arrangement of line segments in the plane and then use it to construct the other star-forests. We will say that the arrangement of <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> line segments is <em id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SF-extendable</em> if the geometric graph with underlying point set consisting of the endpoints of the line segments admits a decomposition into <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests, one of which is the matching given by the arrangement. We say that two line segments are in a <em id="S3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stabbing</em> position if the convex hull of their endpoints is a triangle. If <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=ab</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=cd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=cd</annotation></semantics></math> are two line segments in a stabbing position and the convex hull of <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,b,c,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,b,c,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> in the interior, we say that <em id="S3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> stabs <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math></em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now provide a necessary condition for an arrangement to be SF-extendable.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> be an arrangement of line segments in the plane. If <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is SF-extendable then every pair of segments from <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is in a stabbing position.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume on the contrary that there are two line segments <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="cd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">cd</annotation></semantics></math> which are not in a stabbing position. Then the convex hull of <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,b,c,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,b,c,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a convex quadrangle. Assume that the cyclic ordering of vertices along the convex hull is <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b,c,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b,c,d)</annotation></semantics></math>. The two diagonals of this quadrangle intersect. Assume that <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is SF-extendable. If the star-forest with centers in <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> contains the edges <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,c\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b,d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the star-forest with centers in <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is not planar, contradicting the assumption that <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is SF-extendable. If the star-forest with centers in <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> contains the edges <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a,d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a,d\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b,c\}</annotation></semantics></math> then it is not planar, again contradicting the assumption that <math id="Thmproofx6.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is SF-extendable.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a segment of length <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> with center at the origin. Call its left endpoint <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the right endpoint <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,…,<math id="S3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k},b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k},b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be vertices of some convex <math id="S3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-gon <math id="S3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge of <math id="S3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is placed inside of the top left quadrant of the plane.
We will now construct line segments <math id="S3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2},\dots L_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2},\dots L_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with endpoints <math id="S3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i},b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i},b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. For each <math id="S3.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> place each <math id="S3.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the intersection of interiors of all of the triangles <math id="S3.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{l},b_{l},a_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{l},b_{l},a_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l&lt;j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l&lt;j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math> and the top right quadrant of the plane. We call such a line arrangement a <math id="S3.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase. See Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3 Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for examples of a <math id="S3.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase and a <math id="S3.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase and their extension into a star-forest decomposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an SF-extendable arrangement of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> line segments.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will show that a <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase is SF-extendable. The star-forest with centers in <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i},b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i},b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has the edgeset</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\{a_{i},a_{j}\}:j&gt;i\}\cup\{\{a_{i},b_{k}\}:k&lt;i\}\cup\{\{b_{i},b_{j}\}:j&gt;i\}\cup\{\{b_{i},a_{k}\}:k&lt;i\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\{a_{i},a_{j}\}:j&gt;i\}\cup\{\{a_{i},b_{k}\}:k&lt;i\}\cup\{\{b_{i},b_{j}\}:j&gt;i\}\cup\{\{b_{i},a_{k}\}:k&lt;i\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmproofx7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">It is clear that every edge will be covered by this decomposition. Now we will check planarity of the forest with centers <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i},b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i},b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Edges of the form <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i},a_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i},a_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;i</annotation></semantics></math> cannot cross edges of the form <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b_{i},b_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b_{i},b_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> points are divided from <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> points by the <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-axis. Edges of the form <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i},a_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i},a_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot cross edges of the form <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{b_{i},a_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{b_{i},a_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;i\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;i\leq j</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j},a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j},a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are in different half-planes determined by a line through <math id="Thmproofx7.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i},b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i},b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The other cases are similar.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a complete geometric graph on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> points which can be decomposed into <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\rfloor+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\frac{n}{2}\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2k</annotation></semantics></math> is even, the complete geometric graph is given by the <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase. In the case <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2k-1</annotation></semantics></math>, take a single point away from the <math id="Thmproofx8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase and the resulting complete geometric graph gives the result.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.11044v1/dupla.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:347/119;" width="347" height="119" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Point sets in 3-staircase and 4-staircase configurations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase produces a point set with a fairly large convex hull. So a natural question is to ask if this is in fact necessary or can we find point sets with a smaller convex hull. The following construction shows that we can in fact do this.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We will again construct an arrangment of line segments. Start with a segment of length 1 centered at the origin, and call its endpoints <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},b_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},b_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as before. Now place points <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2},\dots,a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2},\dots,a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in such a way that each <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has a smaller <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and larger <math id="S3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> coordinate than <math id="S3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then place <math id="S3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2},\dots,b_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2},\dots,b_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> so that they obey the same conditions as in the construction of the <math id="S3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase. This way we obtain an arrangement of line segments <math id="S3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},\dots,L_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},\dots,L_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> which is SF-extendable which we will call <math id="S3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-comet. To prove this fact we can use the same star-forest decomposition as we did in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2 ‣ 3 Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. However, where <math id="S3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase defines a point set on <math id="S3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> points with convex hull of size <math id="S3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-comet defines a point set on <math id="S3.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> points with convex hull of size <math id="S3.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3 Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In fact it is not hard to construct such a point set with a convex hull of any size between <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>. First note that the <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-comet is ”obtained” from a <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase by turning a convex polygonal line into a concave one. We say ”obtained” since one also needs to adjust the positions of <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s in the construction. Now we can do the same thing but turn only an initial segment of the convex polygonal line into a concave segement and obtain a smaller convex hull.
Even further, one can make the following observation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 3.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete geometric graph on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2k</annotation></semantics></math> vertices which can be decomposed into <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests, then the size of the convex hull of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmproofx9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproofx9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmproofx9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproofx9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 ‣ 2 Decompositions of Complete Graphs into Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> we know that one of the star-forests will be a perfect matching. If <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> has a convex hull of size at least <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+2</annotation></semantics></math>, then the matching needs to use at least <math id="Thmproofx9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> edges of the convex hull. But then the matching cannot be SF-extendable by Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4 ‣ 3 Decomposing Complete Geometric Graphs into Plane Star-Forests ‣ Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> since the edges of the convex hull are never in a stabbing position.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this we make the following conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> be odd, <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> a complete geometric graph on <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices which can be decomposed into <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil\frac{n}{2}\rceil+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil\frac{n}{2}\rceil+1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests. Then the convex hull of <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> has size at most <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil\frac{n}{2}\rceil+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil\frac{n}{2}\rceil+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">What is not clear is if one can construct point sets with convex hull of size <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> that are not a <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase. Our computations on point sets with <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> points found no such point sets. Thus we make the following conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 3</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If a complete geometric graph <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> points admits a decomposition into <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> plane star-forests and the size of the convex hull of <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+1</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> can be described as a <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-staircase.</p>
</div>
</div>
